# 19. Preliminary National Findings

### Finding 1 — The United States possesses substantial restoration capacity.

Federal, state, Tribal, local, nonprofit, academic, and private institutions collectively provide extensive restoration capability.

The primary issue is therefore not simply institutional absence.

### Finding 2 — Restoration is highly distributed.

No single institution controls the national restoration portfolio.

This creates opportunities for specialization and partnership but complicates national performance measurement.

### Finding 3 — Private lands are indispensable.

Restoration cannot be confined to public lands because many ecological systems cross ownership boundaries.

Programs such as Partners for Fish and Wildlife and NRCS conservation programs demonstrate the importance of voluntary private-land mechanisms.

### Finding 4 — Activity measures remain easier to obtain than ecological outcomes.

Acres restored, miles treated, structures removed, and projects completed are relatively straightforward to report.

Biological recovery and ecosystem-function measures are considerably more difficult.

### Finding 5 — Landscape-scale restoration is increasingly important.

Many ecological processes operate across project and ownership boundaries.

### Finding 6 — Restoration requires diagnosis.

Treating visible degradation without addressing the underlying pressure can produce temporary or incomplete results.

### Finding 7 — Monitoring only creates value when it influences decisions.

A restoration project can be extensively monitored without becoming adaptive.

### Finding 8 — Maintenance is a distinct conservation function.

A restored ecosystem can deteriorate again if the pressure returns or maintenance is discontinued.

### Finding 9 — Restoration and prevention should be evaluated together.

The most efficient conservation strategy may sometimes be to prevent degradation rather than restore it afterward.

### Finding 10 — Restoration should be treated as a performance system.

The ultimate unit of success is not the completed project.

It is the **durable ecological improvement produced by the intervention**.

